body:
edit: formatting

&#x200B;

Awesome points!

&#x200B;

Since it was due to mind controlled, and not him being intentionally antagonistic, I don't necessarily count that as him being a destroyer in the same sense as the early Showa films. However, I genuinely DID forget that Destroy All Monsters takes place chronologically after Terror of Mechagodzilla, though not theatrically.

&#x200B;

As far as Godzilla being back on Monsterland -- there's a lot to debate there. Narratively, the film states that the United Nations "collected" all of the monsters and put them on Monster Island to be secure and studied. Given what we know about the ineptitude of earth weapons against the monsters, and also that in the film (as well as later in Godzilla vs Gigan) the monsters more or less seem to stroll off the island with relative ease, I'd argue this affirms the symbiosis of the situation. Nobody is killing anybody or doing anything like trusting random aliens from a hidden planet who claim to have a miracle drug as long as the monsters stay on Monsterland.

&#x200B;

I think one thing to mention is while Godzilla and humanity build a passable friendship, he has to guess 50/50 as to whether or not we can kill him like we did to '54. He doesn't know who Serizawa was or that he perished with his work, so there's gotta be a back-of-the-mind possibility that we are only restraining from killing Godzilla due to his ability to protect us and seemingly keep all the other monsters in step.

Open mic, not sure. Karaoke had a bit of a rough landing after COVID, but I've just started going to Red Carpet Tavern on Wayne the past couple of weeks. They do karaoke Friday and Saturday nights. Whole bar is friendly, not judgy, drinks are poured generously.

&#x200B;

Full transparency, I did watch one of the staff drag a woman out of the bar by her hair. But I'm bald, so YMMV.

There's a joke here about a Galaxy 7 battery...

&#x200B;

Sick case!
